ead heat (46-47, 45-45)", "Salazar holds a large cash advantage ($2.3M vs $325K)", "Most expert ratings still call the race Lean or Likely R"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Salazar: a -6 partisan lean, incumbency, and a dominant fundraising edge, and most raters still place the race in Republican territory. But two recent Democratic-sponsored polls show a tie or one-point race, and Trump's late-September attacks on Salazar over immigration create a genuine risk to her cross-party coalition, justifying a modest shift toward the Democrat.", "watch": "Whether the Trump-Salazar rift deepens or Salazar consolidates Republican voters; any post-September polling would sharpen the picture.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent María Elvira Salazar (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Representative María Elvira Salazar seeks another term in a Miami-Dade district that leans Republican by about six points, but her position is less comfortable than the map suggests. Two September polls from Democratic-leaning firms show her dead even with challenger Eliott Rodriguez, and she has drawn sustained criticism from President Trump over her immigration stance, an unusual intra-party rift in a district with many Republican-leaning Hispanic voters.\n\nSalazar still holds major advantages: a large cash edge, incumbency, and ratings from most analysts placing the race in Republican territory, though several have moved it toward the competitive column. Markets price her win odds near 60 percent. The race sits on the competitive edge of Lean Republican, with real upside for Democrats if the Trump feud resonates.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2129, "margin": -4.43}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2851, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2818, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3773, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2766, "margin": -3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3218, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026